const HQ_7=[{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Within Rydin's urban politics perspective, planners cannot be neutral technocrats. Statement II: The perspective sees planning processes as potentially disadvantaging the dispossessed and disempowered, so planners need to behave more like advocates.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both statements are Rydin's (p. 105), and II is her reason for I: because planning may disadvantage the dispossessed, neutrality is not an option and planners must act more like advocates. The tempting 'does not explain' misses that Rydin links the two with 'Seeing planning processes as potentially disadvantaging ... means that'."},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims about urban regime theory are consistent with Rydin's chapter? I. Regime power is 'power to' achieve goals, assembled because actors with interdependent resources need each other. II. A regime is a formal, legally anchored partnership that replaces elected local government. III. Pierre argues regimes keep a corporate bias, because leaders must cater to business to access its 'systemic power'.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I and III are in the text (pp. 108–109). II is wrong twice: Stone's regime is 'an informal yet relatively stable group' that works alongside formal politics, not a legal body replacing the council. Options with II tempt students who equate 'stable' with 'formal'."},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: in a Dutch city, an alderman, a housing corporation, two school boards and a large employer form an informal partnership that survives two elections. None of them could achieve its agenda alone, so they pool land, money and staff to expand education and job opportunities for low-income districts. Which concept fits most precisely?","o":["Molotch's growth coalition: actors gaining most from land development unite to push property-led growth","Stone's urban regime: an informal, stable coalition pooling interdependent resources for capacity to act","Castells' urban social movement: residents mobilise over collective consumption against capitalist limits","Trapenberg Frick's tactical coalition: adversaries cooperate on one shared issue while agreeing to disagree"],"a":1,"e":"The decisive features are informality, stability across elections, resource interdependence and 'power to' act; the agenda even resembles Stone's 'lower-class opportunity expansion' type. A growth coalition is tempting, but nothing here is property-led growth by those gaining from land, which was exactly why regime theory replaced that concept."},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"critique","q":"Which pairing of a course perspective with a critique of the urban politics frame (as Rydin presents it) is consistent with that perspective's own logic?","o":["Harvey: regime analysis overstates local agency, since inter-urban competition coerces every coalition","Hayek: the frame neglects how a strong central planner could secure fair outcomes for all groups","Moroni: the frame relies far too little on planners deliberately designing the institutions of the city","Olson: the frame wrongly assumes that disadvantaged groups can never organise at all"],"a":0,"e":"Harvey's structuralism treats inter-urban competition as an 'external coercive law', so stressing coalition agency is exactly his complaint (Rydin notes political economists found growth coalitions 'too agent-based'). The Olson option is tempting, but Olson would say the frame assumes mobilisation too easily, not that it denies it; Hayek and Moroni reject central design, so those critiques invert their logic."},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase most precisely states Rydin's view of what planning can achieve for justice?","o":["Because conflicts stem from capitalism itself, planning can achieve nothing, and justice therefore requires a revolution","Because conflicts are local, planners acting as advocates can secure justice fully within the planning system","Because power is unequal, planners should leave justice to social movements and stay strictly neutral","As conflicts tie into wider power and inequality, planning alone achieves less; justice needs wider political change"],"a":3,"e":"Rydin says views on what planning systems 'on their own can achieve' are 'more nuanced', and that justice can only be fostered 'through wider political change' (p. 107). The first option overstates this into a revolutionary claim (that is closer to Marx), and the third contradicts her statement that planners cannot be neutral."},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"excerpt","q":"A planning report argues: 'Rather than chasing a consensus that hides real differences, the consultation should let the cyclists' union and the shopkeepers' association treat each other as legitimate opponents, cooperate where interests converge, and accept that they will keep disagreeing about car access.' Which idea grounds this passage, and why?","o":["Collaborative planning, because deliberation is expected to bring both groups to a shared, agreed outcome","Dahl's pluralism, because groups form when demand arises and their activism simply follows that demand","Agonism, because conflict is seen as productive and opponents become adversaries who agree to disagree","Post-political planning, because expert knowledge is used to settle the dispute and silence the conflict"],"a":2,"e":"Rejecting consensus, treating opponents as legitimate adversaries and 'agreeing to disagree' are the core of Mouffe's agonism in Rydin (p. 112). Collaborative planning is the tempting option, but the passage explicitly rejects the consensus that collaborative (governance) approaches aim for."},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs Castells' account of urban social movements as Rydin presents it?","o":["Labour must be reproduced, so households need factory wages; conflict therefore centres on the workplace, not on urban services","Labour must be reproduced; households cannot provide housing or transport alone, so collective consumption becomes contested","Labour must be reproduced; the state fully provides all services, so movements only contest the level of local property taxes","Labour must be reproduced; demand for services automatically produces groups, so activism always matches the needs of the city"],"a":1,"e":"Castells links USMs to the 'means of collective consumption': public services needed to reproduce labour that households cannot provide alone, contested against middle-class appropriation and capitalist limitation (p. 110). The last option is tempting because it sounds social, but 'demand automatically produces groups' is the pluralist logic that urban politics rejects."},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"except","q":"Which statement about the research examples in Rydin's chapter is NOT consistent with the text?","o":["Li and Liu supplemented regime theory with 'fragmented authoritarianism' and 'strategic selectivity' for Guangzhou","In Turin, a 'great alliance' moved the city's agenda from a pro-welfare towards a market-centred regime","Fougère and Bond found that antagonism can open space for agonism, while expert knowledge depoliticised","Uysal shows that clear, secure property titles let the state buy out Sulukule residents, splitting the platform"],"a":3,"e":"Uysal lists unclear property rights (not clear titles) among the reasons the Sulukule Platform failed, alongside speculation, lack of public support, lack of resources, fragmentation and counter-pressure (p. 117). The other three statements accurately summarise the Guangzhou, Turin and Denniston Plateau studies."},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"who","q":"Which course author would endorse this claim? 'Groups do not simply form whenever there is demand: resources are unequal, some issues never reach the agenda, and the position of those who gain little can only be understood by also studying the elites who gain most.'","o":["Rydin (2021), urban politics: pluralism is naïve; research must study elites and the disempowered","Hindmoor on Olson: rational individuals free-ride, so large latent groups usually stay unorganised","Davoudi (2006): evidence competes with ideology, interests and institutions in the policy process","Moroni (2010): institutions evolve unintentionally and embody knowledge dispersed among many actors"],"a":0,"e":"The rejection of pluralism for unequal resources and non-issues, plus the joint study of elite and non-elite actors, is Rydin's framing (pp. 105–106). Olson also doubts that demand produces groups, but for a different reason (free-riding by rational individuals), and he says nothing about agendas or elites."},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Rydin presents Stone's urban regime concept as transferring easily to European and Asian cities. Statement II: Regime theory reflects US local government, which relies on local property taxes and has much autonomy from central government.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"II is in the text and is precisely why I is false: because regime theory mirrors US conditions, its transferability is questioned and Holman lists four obstacles, so studies such as Li and Liu on Guangzhou have to amend it (pp. 109, 114). Students who remember the many non-US applications may wrongly accept I."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano call the protection of housing commons 'inherently partial'. Statement II: The federal MCMV-Entidades cap per unit was too low for retrofits in central São Paulo, so state and municipal top-ups were needed.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":1,"e":"Both are in the article, but protection is partial for a different reason: regularization converts collectively claimed public property into individual private titles and debt, opening new routes to enclosure (p. 1035). The funding cap is a practical obstacle to building the commons, not the reason its protection stays partial."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"combo","q":"Which of these definitions of enclosure match the article? I. Commodification: the state gradually absorbs a cooperative into its own administrative logic. II. Marginalization: a self-selecting commons shuts out outsiders through exclusive use, membership or access. III. Cooptation: the state undermines residents' self-governance.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"II and III match the article (p. 1023). I gives the definition of cooptation under the wrong label: commodification means residents selling homes individually for profit, as when a cooperative is dissolved. The trap is that I describes a real form of enclosure, just not commodification."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: the national federation of Dutch housing cooperatives, which groups residents' committees from dozens of projects, lobbies the government until a dedicated loan fund for self-managed cooperatives is written into national housing policy. Which of Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano's patterns of protection does this illustrate?","o":["Integration, which embeds the residents of a project in the political struggle of the wider housing movement","Federation, which links layered commoning bodies to the state and inserts self-management in policy","Regularization, which gives one single housing development legal recognition and a secure tenure","Ideation, in which commons ideas stored in a movement materialise later in a new, different project"],"a":1,"e":"Federation operates at the interface of movement and state: floor and resident committees, entidades, movements and federations lobby until autogestão enters policy, as with MCMV-Entidades (p. 1035). Integration is tempting, but it works between one development and the movement (residents joining the struggle), not between the movement and the state."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"critique","q":"Which pairing of a course perspective with a critique of Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano is consistent with that perspective's own logic?","o":["Harvey: the authors trust anti-capitalist movements too much and should welcome private titles as progress","Olson: points systems cannot work, because selective incentives never sustain cooperation in large groups","Moroni: titles are no new enclosure but secure, general rules letting owners use local knowledge","Dembski and Salet: commons depend only on formal legal titles, not on meanings embedded in mental maps"],"a":2,"e":"An evolutionary-liberal reading (Moroni, after Hayek) values clear, general property rules within which dispersed actors use their own knowledge, so it would reinterpret titling as a gain rather than enclosure. The Olson option is tempting but inverts him: selective incentives are precisely Olson's solution to free-riding, and Harvey would hardly praise private titling."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase most precisely captures the authors' claim about regularization?","o":["Regularization removes the risk of enclosure, because state-sanctioned titles secure the commons permanently","Regularization always destroys the commons, so the authors reject legal recognition of occupations altogether","Regularization prevents eviction, but turning collective claims into private titles opens new enclosure","Regularization creates enclosure mainly because it makes it easier for the state to evict the residents later"],"a":2,"e":"The authors treat regularization as the main defence against eviction while showing that it 'transforms collective use claims to buildings into state-sanctioned private legal claims' (p. 1033), which enables commodification. The first option overstates the protection; the second overstates the harm; the fourth shifts the mechanism from privatisation to eviction."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"excerpt","q":"A housing NGO writes: 'A building is not a commons by nature. It becomes one only when residents produce, share and manage it for its use value, under rules they set themselves, and through living together.' Which framework grounds this passage, and why?","o":["Needham's property-rights view, because the state defines rights that then decide how the land is used","The de Angelis-based commons view in Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano, because commoning practices make the commons","Moroni's evolutionary view, because rules emerge without anyone intending them and are best left untouched","Madden and Marcuse's view, because decommodified housing must be provided by the central state itself"],"a":1,"e":"The article states that housing is not a commons in itself but becomes one through commoning: use value over exchange value, self-set collective-choice and operational rules, and solidarity through cohabitation (p. 1022). Needham is tempting because property matters in the article too, but the passage stresses residents' practices and self-governance, not state-defined rights."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which chain correctly reconstructs why the authors see protection as only partial?","o":["Regularization grants individual titles and mortgages; households become indebted subjects; collective claims become personal debt, eroding collective action","Regularization grants individual titles and mortgages; households become indebted subjects; the movement is then able to enforce its ban on sale and subletting","Regularization grants a collective title to the movement; residents lose security of tenure; they are evicted and the commons is lost to the state","Regularization grants individual titles and mortgages; residents become more dependent on the movement; the movement's control turns into cooptation"],"a":0,"e":"Private ownership plus individual financing creates 'indebted subjects', and converting collective political claims into personal debt-credit relationships 'erodes the potential for collective action' (p. 1035). The second chain fails because the movements cannot enforce the ban (a unit appeared on a subletting platform); the fourth inverts the dependency, since renovation makes residents less dependent on the movement."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"except","q":"Which statement about the São Paulo context and cases is NOT consistent with the article?","o":["Movements target vacant, tax-defaulting or public buildings, which fail the social function of property","MCMV-Entidades gave housing movements direct access to federal housing funds for the first time","After the 2018 fire, the ULC shifted mostly to shorter, political occupations aimed at negotiation","Dandara became an individual condominium, while Marisa Letícia got a collective use-right concession"],"a":3,"e":"The property outcomes are swapped: Marisa Letícia became a condominium with individual titles, and Dandara received an unusual use-right concession (p. 1032). The other three statements are accurate on legitimation strategy, MCMV-Entidades and the ULC's post-2018 tactics."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"who","q":"Which course author(s) would endorse this claim? 'Social rights help movements create and reclaim housing for its use value, but sustaining it depends on how its claims are coupled to state programmes and private law, which both protect it and expose it to enclosure.'","o":["Pruijt (2003): legalization is always safe because squatters' movements are immune to cooptation","Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano (2025): commons need structural coupling, yet private titles reopen enclosure","Harvey (1989): urban entrepreneurialism turns public land and assets into speculative growth projects","Moroni (2010): reform should be modest, gradual, open and limited to general, abstract rules"],"a":1,"e":"The claim paraphrases the authors' conclusion that 'Social rights help to create and reclaim housing as commons, but may not be enough to sustain them' and their stress on the mediating role of private law (p. 1036). Pruijt is tempting because he also studies occupations and the state, but he analyses movement survival under cooptation, not property regimes of the housing."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: In Dandara and Marisa Letícia, units were allocated mainly by need, giving priority to the most vulnerable families. Statement II: The effort-based points system rewarded participation in meetings, occupations and marches, to prevent free-riding.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"II is accurate (p. 1031), and it is exactly why I is false: allocation followed effort, not need. Students who assume a social housing logic of priority by vulnerability may accept I."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Pruijt rejects Castells' (1983) view that institutionalization, with its loss of identity, is an inevitable end for urban movements. Statement II: In Amsterdam, squatters accepted legalization of about 200 buildings yet kept defending squats and rioting, so conventional and disruptive tactics coexisted.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Amsterdam's flexible institutionalization (II) is Pruijt's main evidence that institutionalization need not be terminal (I): legalization in the early 1980s was not an end-of-cycle event (p. 8). Choosing 'does not explain' misses that the Amsterdam case is the empirical ground for rejecting Castells' life-cycle claim."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims match Pruijt's conceptual framework? I. In cooptation, the co-opting organization embraces some movement ideas while redefining problems so that solving them does not threaten its own stability. II. In flexible institutionalization, conventional tactics complement disruptive ones rather than replacing them. III. Post-Fordist theory predicts that a market-oriented regime makes repression of squatters less likely, because cities need squatters as service providers.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I and II are Pruijt's definitions (p. 4). III is wrong: post-Fordist theory predicts stronger pull towards cooptation and stronger push through repression, because competing cities want a clean, safe image (p. 6). The trap is that III mixes a true element (groups as service providers) with a false conclusion."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a Dutch city offers a group of squatters three buildings with renovation money, on condition that they stop organising new occupations and become a foundation that manages temporary housing for the municipality. Which of Pruijt's concepts fits most precisely?","o":["Flexible institutionalization, because the group keeps its disruptive repertoire next to the new lease","Terminal institutionalization, because a squatters' movement ends once its last buildings are legalized","Privatization of poverty, because tenants manage tax-foreclosed buildings with a route to co-op ownership","Cooptation, because the group becomes a service provider inside the city's framework and drops squatting"],"a":3,"e":"Giving activists a managerial role within the co-opting body's framework, in exchange for abandoning squatting, is cooptation, like UK 'licensed squatting' or ACORN in New York (pp. 4–5, 10). Terminal institutionalization is tempting, but it refers to convention replacing disruption through legalization, not to becoming a service provider for the authority."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"critique","q":"Which pairing of a course perspective with a critique of Pruijt is consistent with that perspective's own logic?","o":["Harvey: Pruijt overstates structure and neglects the oppositional identity and the agency of activists","Moroni: Pruijt should have recommended one comprehensive plan to legalize every squat at once","Olson: squatting movements cannot arise, since the housing a squat provides is a pure public good","Raco and Imrie: leases may still govern squatters by shaping them into self-managing, responsible tenants"],"a":3,"e":"A governmentality reading would argue that even a movement 'immune' to cooptation can be governed through its self-management, as leases and rules make squatters conduct themselves responsibly. Harvey's critique would run the other way (too much agency, too little capital), Moroni rejects comprehensive design, and for Olson a squat gives members a private benefit, which helps explain mobilisation."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase most precisely captures Pruijt's explanation of the Lower East Side squatters' movement?","o":["An oppositional identity, partly brought over by European squatters, can partly override the opportunity structure","A pre-existing oppositional identity fully overrides the opportunity structure, so the city's regime type did not matter","The opportunity structure fully determines movement type, so the movement arose because New York chose to tolerate it","The movement arose because the city lost interest in cooptation and therefore negotiated directly with the squatters"],"a":0,"e":"Pruijt says a pre-existing oppositional identity can 'partly' override the opportunity structure (pp. 13–14); he still shows the regime mattered for property law, access and building condition. The last option is wrong: the city refused to negotiate, and its 1992 offer to a Bronx group proves it had not lost interest in cooptation."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"excerpt","q":"A housing activist writes: 'For us, occupying empty buildings is not a tactic we can trade for subsidies or a management contract. Living in squats and defending them together is what our movement is.' Which concept from Pruijt grounds this statement, and why?","o":["A housing movement, because squatting is one possible means to house the poor that can be exchanged","Castells' collective consumption, because the conflict is about public services needed by labour","Flexible institutionalization, because conventional tactics are being added to the disruptive repertoire","A squatters' movement proper, because squatting is means and goal at once, so its goal cannot be shared"],"a":3,"e":"For a squatters' movement 'squatting itself is means and primary goal at the same time' (p. 12), so officials cannot claim to share the goal and offer approved means, which makes it immune to cooptation. The housing-movement option is the mirror image: there squatting is a tradeable means, which is exactly what the activist denies."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs the post-Fordist logic that Pruijt tests?","o":["Welfare costs rise; governments expand public housing to reduce poverty; movements lose their reason to exist and simply dissolve","Welfare costs rise; governments cut spending but still aim to reduce poverty; this pushes them to tolerate squatters as an informal source of housing","Welfare costs rise; governments cut spending but still aim to reduce poverty; they support self-help and co-opt groups as service providers","Welfare costs rise; governments cut spending but still aim to reduce poverty; they nationalise vacant buildings and hand them to squatters"],"a":2,"e":"Facing conflicting demands, governments turn to self-help: the 'privatization of poverty', in which neighbourhood groups are co-opted to plug holes left by the retreating state (p. 5). The second chain is tempting, but post-Fordist theory predicts more repression of squatting in competing cities, not tolerance."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"except","q":"Which statement is NOT consistent with Pruijt's comparison of Amsterdam and New York?","o":["Amsterdam squatters frequently clashed with non-profit developers who claimed the same buildings","A 1971 Dutch Supreme Court ruling extended 'house right' to squatters, enabling long-lived squats","New York squatters concentrated on city-owned buildings, which left private speculators untouched","New York's buildings were in far worse condition, because of a US cycle of disinvestment and arson"],"a":0,"e":"The clashes with non-profit developers happened in New York (six of nine documented Lower East Side conflicts); in Amsterdam a broad consensus meant squatters usually left voluntarily when social housing was scheduled (pp. 19–21). The other three statements are accurate opportunity-structure differences."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"who","q":"Which course author would endorse this claim? 'Whether a city leans on market forces or on redistribution, in line with its welfare-state regime, shapes whether neighbourhood movements are co-opted as service providers.'","o":["Rydin (2021), via Stone: regimes are informal coalitions that pool resources","Raco and Imrie (2000): community is made both object and instrument of rule","Pruijt (2003): a market-oriented regime encourages cooptation of movements","Harvey (1989): inter-urban competition drives cities towards entrepreneurialism"],"a":2,"e":"Pruijt uses 'urban regime' for a city's market or redistributive orientation, grounded in Esping-Andersen's welfare regimes, and finds that a market-oriented regime encourages cooptation (pp. 2, 23). Stone's regime is tempting because of the shared word, but it means an informal governing coalition, not a policy orientation."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Pruijt concludes that a market-oriented regime offers more opportunities for squatting than a regime based on redistribution and planning. Statement II: In New York, the disorganized low-income housing sector gave radical squatters some leeway as watchdogs of the 'poverty industry'.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":3,"e":"Pruijt's 'perhaps paradoxical' conclusion is the opposite of I: a market-oriented regime offers fewer opportunities for squatting (pp. 22–23). II is true, and is the one respect in which the market regime gives more leeway, which may tempt students to generalise it into I."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Raco and Imrie argue that the retreat of the welfare state means less state intervention but not necessarily less government. Statement II: Following Foucault, they treat the sovereign state as the unified, determinate actor from which all governmental power flows.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":2,"e":"I is their central claim (p. 2191). II is false: following Foucault, power works through 'a multitude of agencies and mechanisms not necessarily reducible to the state', which 'cannot be characterised as a unified and determinate actor' (p. 2190). It is precisely because government is not only the state that less intervention can mean as much government."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims about the three elements of governmentality in Raco and Imrie are correct? I. Political rationalities include moral justifications and a picture of who is to be governed. II. Technologies such as audit, benchmarking and devolved budgets create 'calculable spaces' of action. III. Government is a 'problematising' activity that keeps launching programmes to fix its own failures.","o":["I and II only","I, II and III","II and III only","I and III only"],"a":1,"e":"All three are in the framework section (p. 2190): rationalities carry moral justifications and a description of the governed, programmes reflect the problematising character of government, and technologies make conduct calculable. Students who think 'programmes' are only documents may wrongly drop III."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a Dutch municipality lets residents take over a community centre and its budget, but only if they set baselines, report output figures every quarter against targets fixed in advance, and accept that funding stops if they fall short. Which concept fits most precisely?","o":["Stone's urban regime: residents and municipality pool interdependent resources into 'power to' act","Raco and Imrie's communities of obligation: rights to run services come with central duties","Pruijt's flexible institutionalization: conventional tactics complement continuing disruption","Mouffe's agonism: residents and the municipality become adversaries who simply agree to disagree"],"a":1,"e":"The residents gain rights to shape a service only by accepting centrally defined responsibilities and monitoring: Rose's 'communities of obligation' as used by Raco and Imrie, with technologies of audit making their conduct calculable. The regime option is tempting because resources are combined, but nothing here is an informal coalition; it is a conditional, monitored grant from one level of government."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"critique","q":"Which pairing of a course perspective with a critique of Raco and Imrie is consistent with that perspective's own logic?","o":["Harvey: the authors give far too much weight to capital accumulation and class in explaining SRB","Hayek: SRB was too decentralised, so a stronger central plan would have secured better outcomes","Rydin's urban politics: reading policy texts hides how communities contested SRB on the ground","Governmental model: the authors overrate targets and monitoring as useful tools of policy evaluation"],"a":2,"e":"Urban politics stresses conflict, power struggles and the emic view of the marginalised, so it would object that a documentary analysis (no primary fieldwork) shows how texts construct subjects, not how communities resisted or used SRB. The other three invert their perspectives: Harvey would say the authors neglect capital; Hayek rejects central plans; the governmental model values targets and evaluation."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase most precisely states the 'paradoxical assertion' with which Raco and Imrie conclude?","o":["In return for full control over budgets, communities accept some light monitoring by the state","In return for a little extra freedom of action, subjects come under greater monitoring and scrutiny","Communities lose all freedom of action and become passive recipients of centrally designed plans","Monitoring gives communities their freedom, since quarterly data lets residents hold the state to account"],"a":1,"e":"The conclusion reads: 'in return for a little extra freedom of action, subjects come under greater monitoring, scrutiny, and surveillance by the state' (p. 2201). The first option overstates the freedom and understates the control; the fourth reverses the direction of accountability, which the authors find to be vertical, towards funders."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"excerpt","q":"A policy note argues: 'The neighbourhood is the natural level at which problems are understood and solved. Local people know best, so they should bid for funds, build their own baselines and take responsibility for the results.' How would Raco and Imrie analyse this passage?","o":["As government through community: the local gets 'ontological status', becoming object and tool of rule","As genuine collaborative empowerment: participation transfers real decision power from the state to residents","As Ostrom-style self-governance: users set their own rules to manage a common-pool resource without the state","As pluralism: groups form wherever there is demand, so active neighbourhoods simply reflect local interests"],"a":0,"e":"Treating the local as the natural site of effective government, and making communities responsible for bids, baselines and results, is what the authors call government through community, with the local given 'an ontological status' (p. 2194). The collaborative reading is tempting, but it is exactly the empowerment discourse they reinterpret as a technology of government."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs how SRB's technologies work, according to Raco and Imrie?","o":["SRB demands quantified baselines and reports; residents' aspirations become calculable; accountability runs horizontally to residents, who then steer central government","SRB abolishes targets and reports; residents' aspirations become free of expertise; communities replace the state as sovereign over their own regeneration funds","SRB demands quantified baselines and reports; professionals lose all influence; communities ignore national frameworks and set fully independent agendas","SRB demands quantified baselines and reports; residents' aspirations become calculable; accountability runs vertically, so communities govern themselves to central aims"],"a":3,"e":"Quarterly reports against a priori targets make community aspirations 'calculable' and accountability vertical, towards funders, so local actors conduct themselves in line with government objectives: government at a distance (pp. 2197–2199). The first chain is tempting but inverts accountability, which the authors explicitly describe as vertical, not horizontal."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"except","q":"Which statement about SRB is NOT consistent with Raco and Imrie's account?","o":["Ten per cent of project funding had to go to community 'capacity building' work","Best Value disciplined local authorities through standards of value for money and efficiency","Community representatives escaped monitoring because local knowledge was privileged","Even professional experts themselves became subordinated to performance mechanisms"],"a":2,"e":"Local knowledge was privileged only rhetorically: representatives were trained by professionals to set baselines and handle monitoring, so community aspirations became 'calculable' (pp. 2200–2201). The other statements are accurate on capacity building, Best Value and the subordination of professionals."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"who","q":"Which course author(s) would endorse this claim? 'The state is not a unified actor: rule works through many agencies and techniques, and above all through the self-conduct of the governed.'","o":["Foglesong (1986): planning serves capital's collective interest through a relatively autonomous state","Raco and Imrie (2000): government is the 'conduct of conduct' and not reducible to the state","Rydin (2021), governmental model: politicians set goals and expert planners choose the best means","Hayek (1944): central planning shifts decisions to experts, and ultimately to dictatorship"],"a":1,"e":"Raco and Imrie, after Foucault and Dean, see the state as 'but one amongst many institutions' and government as the shaping of conduct, including self-government (pp. 2189–2190). Foglesong is tempting because he theorises the state too, but in Marxist terms of the state managing capitalism's contradictions."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Raco and Imrie argue that SRB rests on 'discourses of public and private sector abandonment'. Statement II: SRB promoted community enterprises and credit unions where services were unattractive to the private sector.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are in the text (p. 2195), and II is the evidence for I: deprived areas left by both state and market are expected to provide for themselves through self-help. Treating them as unrelated misses the logic of the 'communities of obligation' argument."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: On infrastructure funding, the NSW government did not follow the development industry and kept developer contributions. Statement II: On infrastructure, the industry, against its usual anti-intervention line, wanted costs funded from general taxation.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":1,"e":"Both are reported (p. 13), but II describes what industry asked for, not why the government refused: shifting infrastructure costs to the public purse was the one demand the government resisted. Reading II as the explanation confuses the content of a demand with the reason for rejecting it."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"combo","q":"Which claims about the NSW reform process are correct? I. The development industry made the largest share of Green Paper submissions. II. The White Paper made promoting economic growth and development the 'main purpose' of the planning system. III. Despite promising to put people at the heart of planning, the draft Bill curtailed statutory consultation in development assessment.","o":["I and II only","I and III only","II and III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"II and III are findings (pp. 11–12). I is false: the community made 72% of submissions and the development industry only 1%, which is exactly why the authors find the industry's influence on the government's documents so striking. The trap is to equate influence with volume."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"case","q":"Hypothetical: a Dutch minister presents a bill 'to solve the affordability crisis' that forbids municipalities to set requirements that could endanger a project's financial feasibility. In the same bill, the obligation to include a share of social rent in new projects is dropped. Which concept from Gurran and Ruming fits most precisely?","o":["Rhetorical relegation: social goals are moved into subordinate clauses such as 'while' and 'having regard to'","Rent-seeking (Taylor): landowners as insiders lobby to shift a betterment tax onto outsider homebuyers","Growth coalition (Molotch): actors who gain from land development unite with local government for growth","Semantic justification: affordability legitimises deregulation while affordable-housing provisions are cut"],"a":3,"e":"The authors conclude that 'housing affordability problems are used as a semantic justification for a wider deregulatory agenda' (p. 14), shown by the Bill cutting existing affordable housing provisions while banning 'onerous' controls. Rhetorical relegation is tempting, but it concerns the grammar that subordinates goals in a purpose clause, not affordability as a cover for deregulation."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"critique","q":"Which pairing of a course perspective with a critique of Gurran and Ruming is consistent with that perspective's own logic?","o":["Rational choice (Olson, Taylor): concentrated developer interests simply organise and rent-seek better","Raco and Imrie: Gurran and Ruming focus too much on how subjects are formed and neglect organised interest groups","Hayek: the authors are wrong, because planning should pursue one comprehensive goal such as economic growth","Davoudi: the authors expect too little of evidence, since policy should follow research in a linear, instrumental way"],"a":0,"e":"A rational choice reading explains the outcome by small, concentrated groups (developers) overcoming collective action problems and rent-seeking more effectively than dispersed residents, so 'regimes of truth' add little. The other options invert their authors: Raco and Imrie focus on subject formation themselves, Hayek rejects single-goal planning, and Davoudi criticises the linear model."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"nuance","q":"Which paraphrase most precisely captures Gurran and Ruming's conclusion about the reform's outcome?","o":["Industry discourse fully determined the outcome, as the Bill passed and became law largely unchanged","Residents dominated the formal consultation, so industry influence on government documents was marginal","The Bill stalled mainly because the industry turned against it once developer charges were retained","Industry discourse dominated the formal process, yet a backlash outside it helped stall the Bill"],"a":3,"e":"The authors find that industry discourses shaped the White Paper and Bill, but warn it would be 'an over-simplification' to see ideology or interests as the only drivers: the Bill stalled where the government lacked a majority, after the Better Planning Network mobilised outside formal processes (p. 15). The second option reverses who mattered inside the formal process."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"excerpt","q":"A think-tank report argues: 'Every month of delay in approvals adds to the cost of a new home. Councils and objecting residents are the main reason young families cannot afford to buy, so the system must be streamlined.' What does this passage represent in Gurran and Ruming's analysis, and why?","o":["The local-government discourse of distrust, because it blames the undue influence of developers on councils","Urban consolidation, because it argues for containing greenfield growth and densifying existing suburbs","The industry's 'red tape' regime of truth, since planning and residents are blamed for unaffordability","Value capture, because it proposes to levy the uplift from rezoning to fund new, affordable homes"],"a":2,"e":"Framing planning as slow and bureaucratic, residents as part of the problem, and supply constraints as the cause of unaffordability is the industry narrative that became a 'regime of truth' (p. 10). The distrust discourse is the opposite framing, used by local government and residents."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"mechanism","q":"Which causal chain correctly reconstructs the authors' Foucauldian account of how the industry's view prevailed?","o":["A group promotes its version of reality as objective truth; it hardens into a regime of truth; this fixes acceptable problems and solutions, so documents adopt deregulation","A group promotes its version of reality as objective truth; open deliberation tests it rationally; the best-evidenced argument wins, so documents adopt deregulation","A group promotes its version of reality as objective truth; it hardens into a regime of truth; this makes alternative discourses impossible, so resistance can never succeed","A group promotes its version of reality as objective truth; parliament votes on it directly; the majority decides, so the documents adopt deregulation quickly"],"a":0,"e":"Power 'rests on their capacity to promote and sustain a particular version of reality as being the objective truth'; such discourses become 'regimes of truth which work to determine the acceptable formulations of problems and their solutions' (p. 3). The third chain overstates: the authors say regimes of truth can be challenged, and the Bill did stall."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"except","q":"Which statement is NOT consistent with Gurran and Ruming's evidence?","o":["Keeping social housing at its 1996 output level would have turned the estimated shortfall into a modest surplus","Under the unreformed system, approvals reached a 20-year high while Sydney's median price still rose sharply","Australand wanted communities to move from an 'antidevelopment culture' to a 'progressive development culture'","The Property Council supported its claim of a 40% cut in red-tape costs with detailed cost estimates"],"a":3,"e":"The authors stress that for the PCA's 40% claim 'no evidence of actual costs or savings is ever provided' (p. 12). The other three statements are accurate: the NHSC finding (p. 6), the approvals-and-prices irony (pp. 15–16) and Australand's phrase (p. 12)."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"who","q":"Which course author(s) would endorse this claim? 'An analysis of submissions and draft legislation shows how one interest group's framing of housing problems became the government's accepted formulation of problems and solutions.'","o":["Raco and Imrie (2000): policy texts reveal technologies that shape self-governing subjects","Gurran and Ruming (2016): industry narratives became a 'regime of truth' in the draft Bill","Davoudi (2006): evidence-based policy rests on an instrumental, linear view of research","Needham (2012): planning and property rights both protect identifiable private interests"],"a":1,"e":"The claim summarises Gurran and Ruming's aim and finding (pp. 3, 15). Raco and Imrie are tempting because they are also Foucauldian and read policy texts, but they analyse how government shapes subjects through technologies, not how one interest group's framing wins a discursive contest."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"assertion","q":"Statement I: Gurran and Ruming report that housing approvals rose strongly under the unreformed system, while Sydney's median house price kept rising. Statement II: Gurran and Ruming conclude that planning regulation has no effect at all on housing supply.","o":["Both true, and II explains I","Both true, but II does not explain I","I is true, II is false","I is false, II is true"],"a":2,"e":"I is the 'ironic' finding in the conclusion (pp. 15–16). II overstates: the authors question the claim that planning is the main cause of unaffordability and that supply alone restores it, but they do not claim regulation has no effect on supply; their point is about discourse and distribution."}];
const OQ_7=[{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"short","pts":5,"q":"Explain what Stone means by 'power to' as opposed to 'power over', and why this distinction matters for how urban regime theory explains who governs a city.","model":"For Stone, an urban regime emerges because local government cannot implement its agenda alone, so actors with different resources (public authority, capital, expertise) combine them. The power this produces is 'power to': the capacity to achieve goals collectively, not 'power over', the domination of one actor by another (Rydin 2021, p. 108). It works alongside formal politics and is often invisible. This matters because 'who governs' is then not a question of who wins conflicts, but of who is able to assemble the capacity to act; groups whose resources are not needed are 'crowded out'. That is also why critics such as Pierre see a corporate bias: business resources are almost always needed.","rubric":["Defines 'power to' as collective capacity from pooled, interdependent resources","Contrasts with 'power over' as domination of one actor by another","Links it to local government's inability to act alone (Stone, Atlanta)","Draws an implication: governing = assembling capacity; some groups are crowded out or corporate bias (Pierre)"]},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"compare","pts":10,"q":"Compare Rydin's urban politics perspective (2021, ch. 6) with the governmental model of planning (Rydin 2021, ch. 2) on one specific point: the role and position of the planner.","model":"In the governmental model, planning is a rational, linear process: politicians set goals and expert planners choose the best means, with evaluation as a 'critical friend' shadowing the steps. The planner is a neutral technical expert; conflict about ends is handled by politics before planning begins, and the state is a unitary actor pursuing the public interest. The urban politics perspective rejects this neutrality. Because conflict is 'part of the fiber of planning', power is at play within planning processes and outcomes are unequal, Rydin states that 'Planners cannot be neutral in this conceptual context': they must 'behave more like advocates' and consider how to plan justly (p. 105). The planner is therefore situated inside power relations, for example between growth coalitions or regimes and the disempowered. A second difference follows for research: the governmental model produces policy evaluation, while urban politics research is qualitative, normative and emic, often action research supporting marginalised groups (p. 123). In short, one model separates facts (planner) from values (politicians); the other sees the planner's work as itself value-laden and political.","rubric":["Governmental model: neutral expert choosing means for politically set goals","Urban politics: planners cannot be neutral, should act like advocates (quote or close paraphrase)","Explains WHY (conflict, power in processes, unequal outcomes vs consensus/unitary state)","Draws consequence for research or practice (evaluation vs emic/action research)","Stays on the one point asked (role of the planner)"]},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"apply","pts":10,"q":"Hypothetical: a Dutch municipality plans to demolish 600 social-rent homes in a post-war neighbourhood and replace them with mostly owner-occupied housing, together with a housing corporation and a developer who have worked together on several projects. A tenants' platform, supported by students and a migrant organisation, occupies a community centre, makes an alternative renovation plan, and demands to be treated as a partner. Analyse this situation with at least three concepts from Rydin's urban politics chapter.","model":"First, the long-standing cooperation between municipality, corporation and developer can be read with growth coalition and regime concepts. If the partners are driven by gains from land and property, Molotch's growth coalition fits; if they form an informal, stable arrangement that pools land, capital and authority to gain 'power to' act, Stone's urban regime fits better (pp. 108–109). Either way, the tenants' resources are not needed, so they risk being crowded out. Second, the tenants' platform resembles an urban social movement: it mobilises over collective consumption (affordable housing needed to reproduce labour, Castells) and has a physical base in a place (pp. 110–111); like the Sulukule Platform it produces an alternative plan, and Sulukule warns that marginalisation, fragmentation and lack of wider support can still defeat it. Third, Agyeman's justice aspects help evaluate the process: demanding to be a partner is a claim to recognition as a self-determined group, beyond being 'given voice' at a hearing; fair process and fair outcomes (who gets to stay) are also at stake (pp. 106–107). Finally, agonism suggests the municipality should not aim for a false consensus but treat the platform as a legitimate adversary. Planners cannot be neutral here.","rubric":["Uses growth coalition or urban regime correctly for the municipality–corporation–developer alliance","Reads the platform as an urban social movement (collective consumption and/or place base)","Applies Agyeman's justice aspects, especially recognition beyond voice","Uses agonism or 'planners cannot be neutral' to say how planners should respond","Concepts applied to case details, not merely listed"]},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"commentary","pts":15,"q":"Theoretical commentary. An article on Rotterdam's waterfront argues that its redevelopment was driven by an informal but stable alliance of the municipality, the port authority and property investors, who pooled their resources to gain 'the capacity to govern'; tenants' groups were not needed and so were 'crowded out'. (a) Which theoretical perspective from Rydin's urban politics chapter grounds this argument? (b) Critique the argument from an alternative perspective of your choice. (c) What would that alternative perspective recommend to researchers or planners?","model":"(a) The argument rests on Stone's urban regime theory within the urban politics perspective (Rydin 2021, ch. 6). Key signals are the 'informal but stable alliance', pooled resources, and 'the capacity to govern': Stone sees regimes emerging because local government cannot implement its agenda alone, so interdependent actors combine resources and generate 'power to' rather than 'power over'. 'Crowding out' echoes Holman's point that regime analysis looks at less visible connections that 'crowd out' other ways of acting (p. 108).\n(b) From Marxist political economy (Harvey 1989), the argument is too agent-centred, the same critique political economists made of growth coalitions. Harvey would say the alliance is not the explanation but the expression of a structural shift from managerialism to entrepreneurialism: inter-urban competition for mobile capital acts as an 'external coercive law', so any coalition in Rotterdam would pursue speculative waterfront projects, public–private partnerships and the construction of place. The regime account also risks naturalising the corporate bias Pierre identified instead of explaining it by capital's systemic power.\n(c) Harvey would recommend that researchers explain the coalition by the city's position in inter-urban competition and capital accumulation, and that planners resist zero-sum competition through cross-city alliances and a progressive urban corporatism that puts social needs first, rather than hoping a different coalition would change the outcome.","rubric":["(a) Identifies Stone's urban regime theory within urban politics, with evidence from the passage (informal, stable, pooled resources, capacity, 'power to')","(a) Distinguishes it from Molotch's growth coalition or explains 'crowding out'","(b) Critique follows the chosen perspective's logic (e.g., Harvey: too agent-based, external coercive law, entrepreneurialism)","(b) Critique engages with a specific weakness of the argument, not a generic statement","(c) Recommendation consistent with the alternative perspective"]},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"short","pts":5,"q":"Explain what Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano, following de Angelis, mean by 'structural coupling', and why it is double-edged for housing commons.","model":"Structural coupling is de Angelis' term for commons strategically aligning with state systems, such as legal frameworks and funding programmes, to stabilise their claims while trying to safeguard their autonomy (p. 1030). In São Paulo, movements lacked money, so Dandara used SPU Ordinance 179 and Marisa Letícia entered MCMV-Entidades, which gave movements direct access to federal funds for self-managed (autogestão) housing. Without coupling, occupations stay precarious, as Prestes Maia's evictions show. But coupling is double-edged: regularization through state programmes turned collective claims into individual titles and mortgages, creating 'indebted subjects' and opening new routes to enclosure (p. 1035).","rubric":["Defines structural coupling as strategic alignment with state law/funding to stabilise claims","Mentions safeguarding autonomy or self-management (autogestão)","Gives a case example (SPU 179, MCMV-Entidades)","Explains the downside: individual titles/debt, new enclosure (partial protection)"]},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"compare","pts":10,"q":"Compare Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano (2025) with Needham (2012) on one specific point: how they understand property and what it does for the people who hold it.","model":"Needham, from institutional economics of property rights, treats land-use planning and property rights as two ways the state protects identifiable private interests in land: rights are bundles defined by the state, enforced by their holders, and which interests get which protection is a political and technical choice that can be redesigned, for example by creating markets in rights. Property here is a neutral, revisable instrument that allocates entitlements among individuals. Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano also use a relational concept of property as a bundle of claims, rights and obligations shaped by law (pp. 1023–1024), so both reject the idea of property as a simple thing. But they read it politically. Property relations can shield a commons, yet the 'ownership model' that vests power in one entity becomes a tool of enclosure: regularization 'transforms collective use claims to buildings into state-sanctioned private legal claims' (p. 1033) and makes households 'indebted subjects'. Where Needham asks which rights allocation serves interests efficiently, they ask whether a property form preserves use value, self-governance and solidarity. Their call for legal reform enabling collective property echoes Needham's point that rights are redesignable, but for decommodification rather than market efficiency.","rubric":["Needham: property rights as state-defined bundles protecting private interests, redesignable (e.g., markets in rights)","Bossuyt: relational property (claims, rights, obligations) that can protect or enclose","Contrast: efficiency/allocation of interests vs politics of use value and enclosure","Uses a concrete element (individual titles, indebted subjects, legal reform for collective property)","Stays focused on the one point asked"]},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"apply","pts":10,"q":"Hypothetical: in Utrecht, a group of 30 young people founds a housing cooperative in a former office building leased from the municipality. They renovate it with their own labour, set their own house rules and admit members by lottery. Ten years later, several members propose converting the units into individual owner-occupied apartments, because they want to build up equity. Analyse this situation with the concepts of Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano.","model":"At the start the building meets the three dimensions of a housing commons: use value over exchange value (affordable homes, not profit), self-governance (residents set collective-choice and operational rules) and solidarity through cohabitation and shared labour, which resembles mutirão (p. 1022). Several patterns of protection are visible. Restoration: residents made the building habitable themselves. Regularization: the municipal lease gives legal security, a form of structural coupling with the state. Admission by lottery rather than friendship helps avoid marginalization, the form of enclosure in which a commons becomes exclusive. The conversion proposal is a textbook case of commodification: individual sale or ownership for exchange value. The authors would predict this tension, since members of limited-equity cooperatives face a constant temptation of individual gain, and in São Paulo individual titles and mortgages turned households into 'indebted subjects' and eroded collective action (p. 1035). Their analysis suggests protections at higher levels: integration into a wider cooperative movement, federation to lobby for legal forms of collective ownership, and statutes that lock in use value, because a ban the community cannot enforce (as with subletting in São Paulo) is weak protection.","rubric":["Identifies the three dimensions of housing commons in the case","Names at least two patterns of protection correctly (restoration, regularization/structural coupling, integration, federation)","Classifies the conversion as commodification (and lottery as guarding against marginalization)","Links to partial protection / indebted subjects / unenforceable bans","Recommends protection consistent with the authors (collective property law, federation)"]},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"commentary","pts":15,"q":"Theoretical commentary. Excerpt from Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano (2025, p. 1036): 'In trying to understand the stability of housing commons, prevailing institutionalist approaches are only of partial use because they overlook the political dimension integral to such projects.' And: 'Social rights help to create and reclaim housing as commons, but may not be enough to sustain them.' (a) Which theoretical perspective grounds the authors' argument? Support with the text. (b) Critique the argument from an alternative perspective of your choice. (c) What would that alternative recommend for housing policy?","model":"(a) The argument rests on urban commons theory that combines neo-Marxian and institutionalist understandings of the commons, which the authors claim as their contribution. The neo-Marxian side is visible in the stress on the 'political dimension', on enclosure by capital and state (commodification, marginalization, cooptation) and on decommodification: use value over exchange value. The institutionalist side (Ostrom, de Angelis) shows in collective-choice and operational rules, the points system and structural coupling with the state. The second quote shows their relational view of property: public-law social rights (the social function of property) legitimise occupation, but private-law titles decide whether the commons lasts.\n(b) From an evolutionary-liberal perspective (Moroni, building on Hayek), the authors treat individual titles as a failure because they measure outcomes against an ideal of collective use value. Moroni would argue that secure, general property rules are what let dispersed residents use their own knowledge and plan their lives; residents' aspiration to homeownership, which the authors themselves report as a mark of citizenship, is not 'enclosure' but a legitimate choice. Designing collective property to lock residents into a model they increasingly leave also looks like the 'engineeristic' institutional design Moroni warns against, and the evidence base is two success cases chosen on purpose.\n(c) Moroni would recommend modest, general and stable rules (kosmos-oriented) that make many tenure forms possible, including cooperatives, rather than special regimes forcing one model, and would let residents choose between collective and individual ownership.","rubric":["(a) Names commons theory combining neo-Marxian and institutionalist (Ostrom/de Angelis) strands","(a) Supports with the quotes: political dimension, social rights vs sustaining, private law / property","(b) Critique coherent with the chosen perspective (e.g., Moroni/Hayek: general rules, dispersed knowledge, against engineeristic design)","(b) Engages a specific weak point (titles as enclosure, residents' homeownership aspirations, two success cases)","(c) Recommendation follows from the alternative perspective"]},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"short","pts":5,"q":"Explain Pruijt's distinction between a housing movement and a squatters' movement proper, and why it matters for cooptation.","model":"For a housing movement, squatting is one possible means towards a wider goal: housing poor people. For a squatters' movement proper, 'squatting itself is means and primary goal at the same time' (Pruijt 2003, p. 12): it is a bottom-up community with a do-it-yourself ethic and an ideology of self-determination. This matters because cooptation works when officials can claim to share the movement's ultimate goal and offer support if activists switch to approved means, as with ACORN, which got 58 buildings after promising to stop squatting. When squatting is the goal itself, officials cannot credibly share it, so a squatters' movement is immune to cooptation.","rubric":["Housing movement: squatting as one means to house the poor","Squatters' movement: squatting as means and end (quote or close paraphrase)","Explains cooptation logic: shared goal, approved means","Concludes immunity of squatters' movement, ideally with an example (ACORN or Lower East Side)"]},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"apply","pts":10,"q":"In October 2010 the Netherlands made squatting a criminal offence (the 'Wet kraken en leegstand'). Using Pruijt's concepts, analyse what this change means for the opportunity structure of Dutch squatters and what forms of integration or repression you would expect to follow.","model":"Pruijt argues that squatting, like any movement, operates within an opportunity structure that shapes whether and how effectively it can develop. A key element is the legal protection of property. In Amsterdam the 1971 Supreme Court ruling extended 'house right' to squatters, so quick evictions were impossible and long-lived, open squats could flourish; Pruijt notes that the erosion of this protection after 1986 coincided with the movement's decline (pp. 14–15). Criminalisation moves the Dutch situation towards New York, where squatting was criminal trespass. Pruijt's New York evidence suggests what follows: squatters turn to covert action, which isolates them, and to buildings whose owners are least likely to prosecute, leaving speculators untouched (pp. 15–16). In terms of state responses, criminalisation is repression, and Pruijt (after Castells) expects it to be combined with integration. Remaining legalized squats may experience terminal institutionalization if disruption stops, while groups that accept municipal contracts, for instance as managers of temporary housing, would be co-opted. Yet Pruijt's key twist applies: a squatters' movement proper, for which squatting is means and end, cannot be co-opted, so a smaller oppositional core can persist and even keep flexible institutionalization alive.","rubric":["Uses 'opportunity structure' and property protection (1971 house right) correctly","Predicts covert squatting / isolation / changed target buildings using the New York comparison","Distinguishes repression from integration (terminal/flexible institutionalization, cooptation)","Applies the housing movement vs squatters' movement distinction","Concepts tied to the Dutch case, not merely listed"]},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"position","pts":10,"q":"Place Pruijt's argument on the reproduction–transformation dimension. Do urban movements, in his account, end up reproducing the existing order or can they transform it? Justify with the text.","model":"Pruijt sits in a qualified middle position, leaning towards the claim that transformation is possible but limited. On the reproduction side, he confirms that a market-oriented regime 'encourages the cooptation of movement groups as service providers, which in turn implies the abandonment of squatting' (p. 23). Through the privatization of poverty, groups like those in Tenant Interim Lease or Banana Kelly became managers plugging holes left by the state; their energy was absorbed and they stopped challenging the regime. Terminal institutionalization, as in the 2002 Lower East Side legalization, also ends disruption. Even where radicals act as watchdogs, 'conflict tends to be concentrated in a very local arena, leaving big real estate and banking, and big politics comfortably unaffected' (p. 23). On the transformation side, he rejects Castells' claim that institutionalization is inevitable: in Amsterdam, flexible institutionalization let squatters win legalization of about 200 buildings and a voice in planning while keeping disruption, which helped shape a low-income-friendly city. A squatters' movement proper keeps its oppositional identity and is immune to cooptation. So movements can avoid simple reproduction, but whether they transform the city depends on regime and movement type.","rubric":["Takes a clear position (middle, leaning one way) on reproduction–transformation","Reproduction evidence: cooptation, privatization of poverty, terminal institutionalization, local arena quote","Transformation evidence: flexible institutionalization in Amsterdam, immunity of squatters' movement","At least one accurate quote or close paraphrase from Pruijt","Notes conditionality: regime and movement type"]},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"commentary","pts":15,"q":"Theoretical commentary. Pruijt (2003, p. 23) concludes that 'a market-oriented regime encourages the cooptation of movement groups as service providers' and that a squatters' movement 'is immune to cooptation because it involves squatting not only as a means but also as an end'. (a) Which theoretical perspective grounds this argument? (b) Critique it from an alternative perspective of your choice (not governmentality). (c) What would that alternative recommend to researchers or policymakers?","model":"(a) The argument belongs to urban social movement theory with a political opportunity structure approach, tested against post-Fordist theory. The regime (market-oriented versus redistributive, after Esping-Andersen), property law, access to officials and building condition form an opportunity structure that shapes what movements can do; the 'privatization of poverty' explains why market regimes co-opt groups as service providers. Yet Pruijt adds identity and agency: a movement for which squatting is means and end cannot be co-opted, showing 'diverse causal chains leading to similar outcomes'.\n(b) From Marxist political economy (Harvey; Foglesong), the argument is too focused on movements and too little on capital. The housing shortage and the empty buildings that make squatting possible are produced by the circulation of capital in the built environment: disinvestment, redlining and later reinvestment (gentrification) on the Lower East Side. Pruijt himself notes conflict stayed local, leaving 'big real estate and banking' unaffected; a Marxist would say this is not a side issue but the core result: whether co-opted or 'immune', squatters only contest the use of buildings, not the property contradiction (Foglesong) between social land and private ownership. Immunity to cooptation is a cultural victory with little structural effect, and the 'paradox' that redistributive regimes favour squatting reflects how the welfare state manages capitalism's contradictions.\n(c) A Marxist would recommend researchers explain squatting cycles by accumulation and devalorisation of property, and urge movements to build alliances beyond the local housing arena to challenge private land ownership and demand decommodified housing, rather than celebrating autonomy.","rubric":["(a) Identifies USM theory / political opportunity structure, tested against post-Fordist theory","(a) Explains the role of regime type and of identity (means and end)","(b) Critique coherent with chosen perspective (e.g., Marxist: capital, property contradiction, local arena)","(b) Uses a specific element of Pruijt's own evidence or admission","(c) Recommendation follows from the alternative perspective"]},{"t":"raco","fmt":"short","pts":5,"q":"Explain what Raco and Imrie, following Rose, mean by 'advanced liberalism', and why it implies that welfare retrenchment is not a retreat of government.","model":"Advanced liberalism is Rose's term for a mentality of rule that governs 'through regulated choices made by discrete and autonomous actors' in their families and communities (p. 2191). Its features, in Dean's summary, include marketised welfare, audit, fragmented collective provision, a reinvented 'community' and discipline for poor performance. Free, self-determining subjects stand at the centre, but their freedom has to be shaped and guided, for example through empowerment as a 'technology of citizenship'. Therefore the retraction of the welfare state means 'less state intervention' but 'not necessarily ... any less government' (p. 2191): government shifts from direct provision to acting on people so that they govern themselves in line with government aims.","rubric":["Defines advanced liberalism as governing through the self-governing choices of autonomous actors","Names at least two features (audit, marketised welfare, reinvented community, etc.)","Explains the paradox of shaped freedom (e.g., empowerment as technology)","States the 'less intervention, not less government' conclusion with reasoning"]},{"t":"raco","fmt":"excerpt","pts":15,"q":"Excerpt from Raco and Imrie (2000, p. 2197): 'Far from acting as the empowering frameworks, that advocates of advanced liberalism, such as Mulgan (1997), claim, the structuring of rules and procedures in this manner couples together centralised forms of control with decentralised ‘voluntary’ local bids. Local subjects are given a little extra freedom in return for closer monitoring, scrutiny, and subjection to new discourses and the disciplines of external expertise. These technologies of government link the rational objectives of governance with the voluntarily assumed obligations of free agents to make the most of emerging opportunities by conducting themselves responsibly.' (a) Which theoretical perspective do the authors adopt? (5) (b) Support your answer with two arguments, each illustrated with a direct quote from the excerpt. (10)","model":"(a) A Foucauldian governmentality perspective (after Foucault, Rose, Miller and Dean), applied to advanced liberal urban policy.\n(b) Argument 1: power works through technologies that shape conduct, not through direct command. The excerpt speaks of 'technologies of government' and of 'the structuring of rules and procedures' that 'couples together centralised forms of control with decentralised ‘voluntary’ local bids'. This is government at a distance: the centre steers through bidding rules, monitoring and expertise rather than by providing services itself, which is the governmentality triad of rationalities, programmes and technologies.\nArgument 2: government operates through the freedom and self-conduct of subjects. The passage says subjects receive 'a little extra freedom in return for closer monitoring, scrutiny, and subjection to new discourses', and that technologies link governance objectives with 'the voluntarily assumed obligations of free agents ... by conducting themselves responsibly'. This is the 'conduct of conduct': freedom is not the opposite of power but its medium, and responsibilised subjects police themselves. It also explains why the authors reject the claim of advocates such as Mulgan that these frameworks are simply 'empowering': in governmentality terms, empowerment brings no quantitative increase in power but a qualitative transformation of subjectivity, and the 'disciplines of external expertise' make community conduct calculable and open to scrutiny from above.","rubric":["(a) Names Foucauldian governmentality (advanced liberalism) explicitly","(b) Argument 1: technologies/rules steering at a distance, with a verbatim quote from the excerpt","(b) Argument 2: governing through freedom/self-conduct of responsible subjects, with a verbatim quote","Explains HOW each quote reflects the perspective, not just quoting","Quotes are accurate (verbatim from the excerpt)"]},{"t":"raco","fmt":"apply","pts":10,"q":"Hypothetical: a Dutch municipality launches a 'neighbourhood budget' of 200,000 euros per district. Residents' groups can propose projects, but proposals must use a standard template with measurable outputs, be trained by a consultancy in 'impact measurement', report every six months, and are ranked against other districts in a public dashboard. Analyse this scheme with Raco and Imrie's concepts.","model":"Raco and Imrie would read the scheme not as a simple transfer of power but as a new art of government. First, it is government through community: the neighbourhood is treated as the natural site where problems are known and solved, making residents both the object and the instrument of policy (pp. 2193–2194). Second, residents become a community of obligation: they gain rights to propose projects only on condition of taking on responsibilities for delivery and reporting. Third, the template, impact measurement and six-monthly reports are technologies of government that make residents' aspirations 'calculable'; the consultancy training mirrors SRB, where community representatives were trained by professionals to set baselines and monitor (p. 2200). Accountability runs vertically to the municipality, not horizontally to neighbours. Fourth, the public dashboard resembles Best Practice peer review: districts discipline themselves by comparing with others, a form of disciplinary government at a distance (p. 2199). The result is what the authors call 'empowerment' as a qualitative transformation of subjectivity rather than a quantitative increase in power: residents get 'a little extra freedom' in return for closer monitoring. A planner aware of this could reduce reporting demands, allow non-quantified goals and build horizontal accountability to residents.","rubric":["Identifies government through community and/or communities of obligation","Identifies technologies (templates, measurement, reports) creating calculable spaces; vertical accountability","Links consultancy training to reshaped expertise and dashboard to peer discipline (Best Practice)","Uses the empowerment-as-subjectivity or 'little extra freedom' argument","Applies concepts to scheme details rather than listing them"]},{"t":"raco","fmt":"commentary","pts":15,"q":"Theoretical commentary. Raco and Imrie (2000) argue that the SRB's rhetoric of community empowerment is in fact a new art of government in which the state governs 'at a distance' through technologies of audit and the self-conduct of responsible communities. (a) Identify the perspective grounding this argument and its key concepts. (b) Critique the argument from Marxist political economy (for example Harvey 1989). (c) What would that perspective recommend for urban regeneration policy?","model":"(a) The argument rests on Foucauldian governmentality, after Rose, Miller and Dean. Government is the 'conduct of conduct', exercised through political rationalities (community as the site of effective government), programmes (SRB bidding guidance) and technologies (audit, quarterly targets, Best Value, peer review) that create calculable spaces. Advanced liberalism governs through the self-governing capacities of free subjects; 'communities of obligation' gain rights only by accepting responsibilities, and empowerment is a 'qualitative transformation of forms of subjectivity'. The state is 'but one amongst many institutions'.\n(b) From Harvey's Marxist political economy, the analysis describes the techniques of rule well but leaves unexplained why they appeared and whom they serve. Harvey would read SRB as part of the shift from managerialism to entrepreneurialism: competitive bidding, public-private partnerships and the abandonment of deprived areas reflect inter-urban competition and the needs of flexible accumulation, which act as an 'external coercive law'. Raco and Imrie mention 'discourses of public and private sector abandonment', but treat them as discourse rather than as the result of capital withdrawing and the fiscal crisis of the state. By decentring the state and class, governmentality risks becoming descriptive: it shows how subjects are shaped, not the material interests behind regeneration, nor regressive redistribution towards capital. Their documentary method also cannot show material outcomes.\n(c) Harvey would recommend analysing regeneration through flows of capital, land values and distributional outcomes, and replacing zero-sum competitive bidding with needs-based redistribution and cross-city alliances (a progressive urban corporatism) instead of making poor communities compete for minimal funds.","rubric":["(a) Names governmentality and explains at least three key concepts accurately (rationalities/programmes/technologies, advanced liberalism, communities of obligation, government at a distance)","(b) Critique coherent with Marxist political economy (capital, class, entrepreneurialism, inter-urban competition)","(b) Engages with a specific feature of the article (abandonment discourse, decentred state, documentary method)","(c) Recommendation follows from Harvey's logic (redistribution, against competitive bidding, alliances)","Clear structure (a)(b)(c)"]},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"short","pts":5,"q":"Explain what Gurran and Ruming mean by 'rhetorical relegation' of social and environmental goals, using the wording of the NSW reform documents.","model":"Rhetorical relegation (a label for the authors' observation) is the way a text subordinates some goals by grammar. The White Paper states that 'The main purpose of the planning system is to promote economic growth and development ... while protecting the environment and enhancing people's way of life', and the draft Bill tells strategic plans to facilitate housing and other 'economic activity, having regard to environmental and social considerations'. The authors conclude: 'Environmental and social concerns are thus relegated to secondary or ancillary positions through terms such as “while” and “having regard to”' (p. 11). It matters because it shows the industry's 'economic dividend' framing becoming the official purpose of planning, while other goals stay present only formally.","rubric":["Explains subordination of goals through wording","Cites 'while' and/or 'having regard to' from the documents","Links to growth as the 'main purpose' / housing as 'economic activity'","Connects to the industry discourse or regime of truth"]},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"compare","pts":10,"q":"Compare Gurran and Ruming (2016) with Davoudi (2006) on one specific point: the role of evidence in policymaking.","model":"Davoudi criticises the UK enthusiasm for evidence-based policy as resting on an instrumental, linear and utilitarian view of research, the same naive assumptions as positivist planning in the 1960s and 70s. In practice evidence competes with ideology, interests, institutions and prior information, so she proposes an enlightenment model: evidence-informed policy, where research gradually shapes how problems are understood. Gurran and Ruming offer a striking case that confirms her point, but go further through Foucauldian discourse analysis. In NSW, the Property Council claimed a 40% cut in costs from red-tape reduction while 'no evidence of actual costs or savings is ever provided', yet the draft Bill banned 'onerous' controls (p. 12). Meanwhile, evidence that cut against the dominant narrative, such as the National Housing Supply Council's finding that the shortfall would have been a surplus if social housing output had stayed at 1996 levels, was ignored. For Gurran and Ruming, what counts as true is produced in power struggles: a group's capacity 'to promote and sustain a particular version of reality as being the objective truth' creates a 'regime of truth' (p. 3). So Davoudi sees evidence as one input among competing influences and hopes to improve its use; Gurran and Ruming see 'evidence' itself as part of the discursive contest.","rubric":["Davoudi: critique of linear/instrumental evidence-based policy; evidence competes with ideology, interests, institutions; enlightenment model","Gurran: evidence-free claims (40%) adopted; contrary evidence (NHSC) sidelined","Gurran: truth produced through discourse and power (regime of truth)","Clear statement of similarity AND difference","Stays on the one point (role of evidence)"]},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"apply","pts":10,"q":"Hypothetical: during a Dutch housing shortage, a coalition of developers and builders publishes a manifesto saying that 'procedures and objection rights are the main reason housing is unaffordable'. The government then drafts a law that shortens objection periods, limits municipal requirements on sustainability and design, and presents this as the solution to affordability, while housing associations and tenants' groups warn about rising rents. Analyse this situation with Gurran and Ruming's concepts.","model":"Gurran and Ruming would treat the manifesto as an attempt to establish a 'regime of truth': a group's capacity to present its version of reality as 'objective truth' determines which formulations of problems and solutions count as acceptable (p. 3). Here the problem is framed as procedures and objectors, mirroring the NSW industry narrative of planning as red tape and residents as part of the problem. If the government's draft law adopts this framing, the discourse has moved from submissions into legislation, as with the White Paper and draft Planning Bill. Presenting deregulation as the solution to affordability, while ignoring rents and social housing, looks like a 'semantic justification': affordability legitimises a wider deregulatory agenda (p. 14). Limiting sustainability and design requirements resembles the NSW ban on 'onerous' controls that affect financial viability, and curtailing objection rights resembles the reduced consultation in development assessment. The authors would ask for evidence: in NSW, approvals reached a 20-year high under the old system while prices still rose, and the supply gap reflected the retreat of social housing. Finally, they would note that a regime of truth is not all-powerful: tenants' groups, housing associations and municipalities can contest it, inside parliament or through mobilisation outside formal processes, like the Better Planning Network.","rubric":["Identifies the manifesto as a (potential) regime of truth with correct definition","Identifies semantic justification of affordability (and/or rhetorical relegation, 'onerous' controls)","Uses NSW evidence (approvals vs prices, NHSC/social housing) to question the supply claim","Notes contestation outside formal processes (Better Planning Network)","Applies concepts to case details rather than listing them"]},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"commentary","pts":15,"q":"Theoretical commentary. Gurran and Ruming (2016, p. 16) conclude that what emerges from their study 'is the capacity of development interests to influence policy settings by mobilising discourses, as regimes of truth, which characterise the planning system as a drain on economic growth, and a constraint to housing supply'. (a) Which theoretical perspective grounds this argument? Support with the text. (b) Critique it from rational choice theory (for example Olson, or Taylor's rent-seeking analysis). (c) What would rational choice theory recommend?","model":"(a) The argument rests on a broadly Foucauldian discourse analysis, which the authors combine with a critique of neoliberal planning reform. Language is 'central in producing or constructing reality', and a group's power lies in its capacity to sustain its version of reality as the 'objective truth'; such discourses become 'regimes of truth' that determine acceptable problems and solutions (p. 3). The quote applies this: development interests mobilise discourses (planning as drain, constraint), which then appear in the White Paper's growth purpose and in the draft Bill's ban on 'onerous' controls.\n(b) From rational choice, the discourse is not the explanation but the visible surface of interest-group competition. Olson predicts that small groups with concentrated stakes (developers, peak bodies) organise more easily than large, dispersed groups (renters, aspiring buyers), and Taylor shows Melbourne landowners rent-seeking successfully through public submissions. Read this way, the 1% of industry submissions punched above their weight because of resources, access and concentrated benefits, not because of a 'regime of truth'. The Better Planning Network's success also fits: homeowners have concentrated stakes in their own neighbourhoods and electoral leverage. The Foucauldian account adds little explanatory power and does not specify mechanisms of influence; it also never tests whether regulation raises costs.\n(c) Rational choice would recommend reducing scope for rent-seeking: transparent, rule-based decisions (fewer discretionary spot rezonings), clear property rights and value capture through general rules, and lowering transaction costs so dispersed groups such as renters can organise, rather than analysing discourse.","rubric":["(a) Names Foucauldian discourse analysis / regimes of truth, with accurate textual support","(a) Links the quote to the documents (growth purpose, 'onerous' controls, semantic justification)","(b) Critique coherent with rational choice (concentrated vs dispersed interests, free-riding, rent-seeking)","(b) Uses specific evidence from the article (1% submissions, Better Planning Network, missing test of cost claims)","(c) Recommendation follows rational choice logic (rules, transaction costs, limiting rent-seeking)"]}];
const NQ_7=[{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"define","q":"In Agyeman's framework of justice, what does the aspect of 'recognition' involve?","o":["Giving every group a chance to speak at public hearings","Ensuring outcomes meet the needs of different groups fairly","Recognising their claims as self-determined groups","Fair exercise of regulation and the implementation of decisions"],"a":2,"e":"Recognition 'goes beyond simply giving voice to a group': it means recognising their claims as a self-determined group (pp. 106–107). The first option is the tempting understatement; the other two are Agyeman's separate aspects of fair outcomes and just procedures."},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"example","q":"Which case does Rydin use to show environmental justice as a struggle over Indigenous rights and decolonisation?","o":["Native American fishing rights after the 1974 Boldt decision","The Love Canal housing development on top of a former landfill site","The Sulukule regeneration of a largely Roma district in Istanbul","The Denniston Plateau coal mine consent process in New Zealand"],"a":0,"e":"Miller Cantzler and Huynh studied fishing rights after the Boldt decision, using distributive, procedural and recognition justice and stressing Indigenous agency (pp. 117–118). Love Canal is the trigger case for US environmental justice in general, not for Indigenous rights."},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"recall","q":"In Mayer's four phases of urban social movements, which phase does she date to 1990–2000?","o":["Industrial crisis and new movements","Welfare erosion and collective consumption","Globalisation and financialisation","Gentrification and urban regeneration"],"a":3,"e":"Mayer's sequence is industrial crisis (1960–80), welfare erosion and collective consumption (1980–90), gentrification and regeneration (1990–2000), and globalisation and financialisation since 2000 (p. 111). Students often swap the last two."},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"not","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of Stone's four urban regime types?","o":["Caretaker regime","Entrepreneurial regime","Middle-class progressive","Lower-class opportunity expansion"],"a":1,"e":"Stone's four types are maintenance/caretaker, development, middle-class progressive and lower-class opportunity expansion (p. 109). 'Entrepreneurial' sounds plausible because of Harvey's entrepreneurial city, but it is not one of Stone's regime types."},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"apply","q":"Hypothetical: environmental groups and tax-cutting conservatives briefly join forces to defeat one road-tax referendum, then go their separate ways. In Trapenberg Frick's typology this coalition is:","o":["Ephemeral","Emergent","Established","Regime-like"],"a":0,"e":"Trapenberg Frick distinguishes ephemeral, emergent and established tactical coalitions (pp. 119–120); a one-off alliance that dissolves after a single vote is ephemeral. 'Regime-like' is wrong because regimes are relatively stable and long-lived."},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"contrast","q":"Why did Li and Liu adapt regime theory when studying Guangzhou's Three Old Redevelopment policy?","o":["Business was so dominant that a growth coalition model fitted better","Local councils in China rely on property taxes exactly as US cities do","Business is weaker and civil society thinner in China than in the US","Residents had formed a strong urban social movement that blocked all projects"],"a":2,"e":"Li and Liu preferred regime theory to growth coalitions but added 'fragmented authoritarianism' and 'strategic selectivity' because the Chinese state is dominant, business weaker and civil society thin (p. 114). This is an instance of the transferability problem."},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"recall","q":"How does Rydin characterise research in the urban politics perspective in her conclusions?","o":["Mainly quantitative, detached and aimed at testing hypotheses about power","Mainly modelling of actors' rational strategies and their incentives","Mainly historical analysis of critical junctures and path dependency","Mainly qualitative, normative, often action research, from an emic view"],"a":3,"e":"Rydin stresses qualitative methods, a normative push towards supporting the groups studied, and the emic perspective of the disempowered (p. 123). Statistics can help show inequality, but detached hypothesis testing is not how she characterises the field."},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"recall","q":"Why, according to Rydin, did left-leaning theorists turn to urban social movements?","o":["They believed pluralism had shown that all groups enjoy equal access to power","Disheartened that political economy made change hinge on crisis and revolution","They wanted to show that markets allocate public services more efficiently","They found growth coalitions far too structural and in need of more agency"],"a":1,"e":"Theorists disheartened by political economy's message sought a more incremental route, treating politics as relatively autonomous from capitalist structure, inspired by Gramsci (pp. 109–110). The last option inverts the critique: political economists found growth coalitions too agent-based."},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"example","q":"What shift did the 'great alliance' studied by Ravazzi and Belligni bring about in Turin?","o":["From a pro-welfare towards a market-centred urban agenda","From a market-centred towards a welfare-oriented agenda","From central state control to an urban social movement","From a growth coalition to an agonistic tactical coalition"],"a":0,"e":"Centre-left politicians, liberal business leaders (the Group of 70) and Politecnico academics pushed Turin towards housing, infrastructure, a knowledge district and tourism: a move from pro-welfare to market-centred governance, built through naming, blaming and claiming (pp. 113–114)."},{"t":"rydin6","fmt":"define","q":"In the chapter, what does 'post-political' planning refer to?","o":["Planning after a revolution has removed class conflict","Planning that annuls dissent and silences overt conflict","Planning by social movements without any role for the state","Planning that treats every conflict as productive"],"a":1,"e":"Agonism is presented as resistance to 'the silencing of conflict' in a post-political society (Raco 2014), and Fougère and Bond show expert knowledge keeping a process post-political (pp. 112, 121–122). The last option describes agonism itself, the opposite."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"not","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three dimensions of a housing commons in the article?","o":["Use value prioritised over exchange value","Self-governance by the residents","Individual ownership of each unit","Solidarity through cohabitation"],"a":2,"e":"The three dimensions are decommodification (use over exchange value), self-governance and solidarity through living together (p. 1022). Individual ownership is what regularization produces, and it is a route to enclosure, not a dimension of the commons."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"recall","q":"How do de Angelis and Stavrides, as cited by the authors, think commons are best protected?","o":["Through porosity and proliferation","Through strict closure and exclusion","Through state ownership of all land","Through market pricing of each unit"],"a":0,"e":"They take a middle ground between Dardot and Laval (commons cannot be property) and Harvey ('some degree of closure'): commons survive through porous boundaries and by spreading beyond themselves (p. 1023). 'Closure' is Harvey's position."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"define","q":"Which three elements make up de Angelis' view of a commons, as used in the analytical framework?","o":["Land, labour, capital and the state","Common good, community and commoning practices","Rights, responsibilities and audit regimes","Appropriation, regularization and renovation stages"],"a":1,"e":"The framework uses de Angelis' tripartite commons: the common good (object, including relational property), the community (subject) and commoning practices (pp. 1023–1024). The last option lists stages of the cases, not elements of a commons."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"recall","q":"How did Bossuyt and D'Ottaviano gather their evidence?","o":["A survey of 1,000 occupiers across São Paulo","Participatory action research plus 19 interviews","Statistical analysis of national census data only","A discourse analysis of newspaper coverage"],"a":1,"e":"From 2016 to 2021 the team did participatory action research through the Brazilian extensão model (one member was Dandara's architect) and held 19 interviews with residents, leaders, policymakers, social workers and an architect (p. 1024)."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"example","q":"Why did occupiers immediately clean, change locks and set access rules after entering a building?","o":["Because the federal programme required it before any funding","Because the owners paid them to maintain the property","Because care also built legitimacy against 'invasion' claims","Because social workers ran compulsory condominium courses"],"a":2,"e":"Care in the appropriation stage was also strategic: any sign of degradation feeds hostile politicians, and residents insist they occupy rather than 'invade' (p. 1029). Condominium courses came only at the moving-in stage."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"define","q":"In the cases, what does 'mutirão' refer to?","o":["A federal funding scheme for movements","Collective labour by residents in construction","A legal title for use rights in public buildings","Nightly guarding of the site before move-in"],"a":1,"e":"Mutirão is collective labour in which residents contribute to building and renovation, alongside committees and autogestão (pp. 1030–1031). Night vigils are part of restoration, and the use-right concession is Dandara's property form."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"example","q":"What does the history of the Prestes Maia building illustrate in the article?","o":["How appropriation stays precarious without state coupling","How a commons survives through porosity without any funding at all","How a fire led the ULC to prefer short political occupations","How a cooperative was dissolved and its units sold for profit"],"a":0,"e":"Prestes Maia went through three occupations and two evictions before renovation became possible in 2022 through the municipal Pode Entrar programme (p. 1028). The fire that changed ULC tactics was in the Wilton Paes de Almeida building."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"recall","q":"What do the authors call for in their conclusion?","o":["Abolishing private property in housing across the whole of Brazil at once","Selling the completed units to fund a new wave of occupations","Leaving commons to regulate themselves without any state role","Legal reform for collective property and an 'archipelago of the commons'"],"a":3,"e":"Because Brazil lacks collective property arrangements, they call for legal reform to support housing commons against marketization, and for mapping an 'archipelago of the commons' of mutually supporting projects (pp. 1036–1037). They stress private law's progressive potential rather than abolition."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"apply","q":"Why can the pattern of 'integration' clash with what many residents want?","o":["Residents are legally barred from joining any housing movement","Many residents aspire to homeownership as a mark of citizenship","The state forbids movements from allocating the housing units","Residents prefer that the city run all the buildings directly"],"a":1,"e":"Homeownership is widely seen in Brazil as a precondition for full citizenship and a symbol of stability (p. 1027), which pulls against the collective commitment that integration and the points system cultivate (p. 1034)."},{"t":"bossuyt","fmt":"inverse","q":"Which observation from the fieldwork best illustrates enclosure through commodification?","o":["Gates and doormen were installed to control access","Residents slept on the mezzanine to guard the site","A unit was listed on a subletting platform","Social workers ran courses on condominium life"],"a":2,"e":"Late in the fieldwork a unit appeared on a subletting platform, despite the movements' ban on selling and subletting (p. 1036): exchange value creeping back in. Gates and vigils are restoration (protection), not enclosure."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"inverse","q":"Which example does Pruijt use as the classic case of cooptation of squatters?","o":["The 1977 'amnesty' for London squatters","Amsterdam's purchase of about 200 squatted buildings","The 2002 legalization of eleven squats in New York","UK 'licensed squatting' of short-life housing"],"a":3,"e":"In licensed squatting, squatters' groups became offices managing short-life public housing and had to stop organising squatting, which split the movement (pp. 4–5). The London amnesty and the 2002 legalization are forms of institutionalization, not cooptation."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"recall","q":"How does Pruijt, following Esping-Andersen, characterise the welfare regimes of the two countries?","o":["US liberal; Netherlands with social-democratic traits","US social-democratic; the Netherlands fully liberal","Both corporatist, differing only in their city sizes","US conservative; the Netherlands fully socialist"],"a":0,"e":"The US is a liberal regime that encourages the market, while the Netherlands has social-democratic traits (pp. 6–7). This regime contrast is what makes the two cities a most-different comparison on the opportunity structure."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"example","q":"What happened in New York's official 'homesteading' programmes, according to Pruijt?","o":["Homesteaders were evicted within a few weeks by the city police","Squatters gained legal 'house right' over vacant buildings","The city sold all the buildings to speculators at public auction","Homesteaders came to see themselves as owners opposing squatters"],"a":3,"e":"Groups renovated abandoned city buildings with 'sweat equity', often over six to ten years, and gradually saw themselves as homeowners opposed to squatters, which split the anti-displacement movement (pp. 9–10). 'House right' is the Dutch 1971 ruling."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"recall","q":"Why can't the city's lack of interest in cooptation explain why Lower East Side squatters were never co-opted?","o":["Because the squatters themselves asked the city for funding","Because the city had already legalized all the squats by 1985","Because in 1992 it tried to co-opt a Bronx squatting group","Because Amsterdam pressured New York to stop co-opting groups"],"a":2,"e":"In 1992 the city offered a Bronx group legalization of three buildings if the other squatters left and it became a service provider, so the city was still pursuing cooptation (pp. 11–12). The explanation therefore has to lie in the movement itself."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"not","q":"Which was NOT one of the features of the broad Amsterdam consensus on low-income housing that Pruijt describes?","o":["Affordability, with 'building for the neighborhood'","A clear line between profit and non-profit building","Squatters agreeing to manage short-life city housing","Recognition of squatters' own particular housing needs"],"a":2,"e":"The consensus rested on affordability, a profit/non-profit demarcation, reliable and transparent plans, democratic planning and recognition of squatters' needs (pp. 21–22). Managing short-life housing is the UK licensed-squatting model of cooptation."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"define","q":"What does Pruijt mean by the 'Weber-Michels' scenario?","o":["Co-opted groups drifting into managerial self-interest","Squatters turning into gentrifiers of their own neighbourhood","The state repressing all squatting through new criminal laws","Movements federating into a national lobby organisation"],"a":0,"e":"It is the drift 'from social movement to managerial self-interest focused organization'; radicals can then act as watchdogs and expose the privatization of poverty (p. 23)."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"recall","q":"Why might authorities prefer legalizing squats over evicting them, according to Pruijt?","o":["Because squatters always pay higher rents than regular tenants","Because the law forbids any eviction of squatters whatsoever","Squatting enjoys some legitimacy, and eviction can be too costly","Because legalized squats automatically become owner-occupied"],"a":2,"e":"Authorities may opt for legalization because squatting enjoys some legitimacy and eviction may be too costly; squatters are drawn to it because squatting is also an economic activity meeting housing needs (p. 3)."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"example","q":"What does the trajectory of Banana Kelly in the Bronx illustrate?","o":["A squatters' movement proper that stayed immune to cooptation","Flexible institutionalization with continued disruption","Gentrification caused directly by squatting artists","A move from squatting to service provision, then scandal"],"a":3,"e":"Banana Kelly evolved from squatting to renovation to an award-winning service provider, then collapsed in scandal and poor maintenance (pp. 10–11): cooptation as post-Fordist theory predicts, with Weber-Michels risks."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"recall","q":"How does Pruijt partly explain why Amsterdam's squatters' movement could grow so large?","o":["A 'lost generation' of youth with time, welfare and grants","Generous city subsidies paid directly to the squatting groups","Stronger legal protection for owners than in the United States","A national plan that assigned squats to the homeless first"],"a":0,"e":"The movement took thirteen years to develop and was fed by a 'lost generation' with few jobs but time, welfare payments and student grants (p. 23). Owners were less protected in the Netherlands, not more."},{"t":"pruijt","fmt":"define","q":"What does Pruijt count as 'effective squatting'?","o":["Squatting that ends in individual home ownership for all of the squatters","Squatting that is fully legalized within a single year of the occupation","Viable models for all building types, plus a low-income-friendly city","Squatting that avoids any contact with owners, authorities or neighbours"],"a":2,"e":"Effective squatting means viable models (repair, heating, dealing with owners, authorities and neighbours) for every type of empty building, plus a contribution to a lively, low-income-friendly city (p. 2). Avoiding contact is the opposite: covert squatting isolated New York squatters."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"recall","q":"What did Blair's phrase 'something for something' express, according to Raco and Imrie?","o":["Unconditional social rights for all","Rights granted in exchange for duties","Market pricing of all public services","Full devolution of power to councils"],"a":1,"e":"The rights and responsibilities agenda: 'You only take out what you put in. That's the bargain' (p. 2187). Citizens' rights become conditional on fulfilling obligations."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"example","q":"Which policy do Raco and Imrie cite as a 'technology of citizenship' that turns powerless subjects into active citizens?","o":["The Single Regeneration Budget's 10% rule","Best Value in local government","Tenant participation guidance","The New Deal for the unemployed"],"a":3,"e":"The New Deal combined counselling to raise self-esteem with training, which the authors, following Cruikshank, read as empowerment used as a technology of citizenship (p. 2191)."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"define","q":"What are 'calculable spaces' in Raco and Imrie's analysis?","o":["Domains made governable through baselines, outputs, milestones","Areas where land values are calculated for betterment taxes","Places where communities freely set their own local priorities","Zones in which market competition replaces public provision"],"a":0,"e":"Technologies of government such as audit and benchmarking create 'calculable spaces' in which conduct can be measured against authoritative criteria and monitored from above (p. 2190)."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"apply","q":"SRB bidders adjusted their conduct to the scrutiny of other bidders through 'Best Practice' guidance. What does this illustrate?","o":["Horizontal accountability to the local residents","Disciplinary government at a distance via peers","Market competition replacing all regulation","Agonistic conflict between rival partnerships"],"a":1,"e":"Best Practice adds peer review: bidders discipline themselves by comparing with others, another form of government at a distance that needs no direct central order (pp. 2199–2200)."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"recall","q":"What did the requirement that bids 'add value' and 'work within the grain' of national frameworks do, according to the authors?","o":["It freed communities from national priorities","It made central government liable for local failures","It allowed bidders to ignore other programmes","It put the burden of policy coherence on recipients"],"a":3,"e":"Bids had to fit national frameworks and add value to other programmes, placing the burden of policy coherence on recipients rather than policymakers, which limits autonomy (p. 2195)."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"recall","q":"How do Raco and Imrie end their article?","o":["By calling for a return to Keynesian welfare provision","By arguing that what is constructed can be reconstructed","By recommending SRB as a model for other countries","By concluding that all resistance to government is futile"],"a":1,"e":"Because the art of government is politically constructed, it can be reconstructed on different principles; reflexive government may open space for a 'subpolitics' of identity and difference (p. 2202). They do not end in pure pessimism."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"contrast","q":"What, according to Raco and Imrie, is new about New Labour's 'active citizen' compared with Thatcherism?","o":["Active citizenship becomes central to state–civil society ties","Active citizenship is invented here for the very first time","Citizens are defined mainly as consumers of public services","The state takes back responsibility for all welfare provision"],"a":0,"e":"Thatcherism already encouraged voluntary action as the state shrank; what is new is that active citizenship moves to the centre of the relation between state and civil society, with citizens cast as democratic agents (p. 2188)."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"example","q":"What did Foley et al.'s evaluations of SRB projects find, as cited by Raco and Imrie?","o":["Monitoring data were used mainly to improve policy design","Communities rejected all central targets and set their own","Trust between partners grew as monitoring was reduced","Surveillance replaced trust: 'an inspection mentality'"],"a":3,"e":"Systems of surveillance and management discipline were replacing relations of trust, and 'An inspection mentality can start to dominate'; monitoring was used to check targets rather than to improve policy (pp. 2197–2198)."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"inverse","q":"The Dutch shift to a 'participatiesamenleving' (2013) is best read through Raco and Imrie as:","o":["A transfer of sovereignty from the state to the citizens","Responsibilisation: governing through active citizens","A return to the postwar Keynesian welfare state model","An urban regime pooling business and city resources"],"a":1,"e":"Asking citizens to take more responsibility for their own lives and neighbourhoods matches the governmentality of active citizenship: less state intervention but not less government. Reading it as a transfer of sovereignty is the naive empowerment view the authors criticise."},{"t":"raco","fmt":"not","q":"Which is NOT among the features of advanced liberalism listed by Dean, as summarised by Raco and Imrie?","o":["Marketised welfare and consumerism","Audit and discipline of poor performance","Expansion of universal state services","A reinvented notion of 'community'"],"a":2,"e":"Advanced liberalism involves marketised welfare, audit, fragmentation of collective welfare, a reinvented community and discipline for deviant performance (p. 2190, note). Expanding universal state services runs against its logic."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"define","q":"What does 'urban consolidation' mean in the Australian context of the article?","o":["Merging small councils into larger ones","Selling public housing to sitting tenants","Deregulating development assessment","Curbing greenfield spread and densifying"],"a":3,"e":"Urban consolidation is Australia's term for growth management: limiting greenfield spread and densifying existing areas. Industry opposed it for limiting land supply, homeowners for bringing density (p. 5)."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"recall","q":"What happened to spot rezoning in the NSW reform proposals?","o":["Rezoning was eased, with developer appeals, and adopted","Rezoning was banned to protect local plans and heritage","Rezoning required a referendum among local residents","Rezoning was handed to Shelter NSW for affordable homes"],"a":0,"e":"The Green Paper made spot rezoning easier and let developers appeal refusals; industry supported this, councils and communities opposed it, and the White Paper adopted it in the name of housing supply (p. 11)."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"example","q":"Which submitter framed planning as a mechanism for 'bulldozing the beautiful'?","o":["The Property Council of Australia","The Urban Development Institute","Friends of Ku-ring-gai Environment","The Planning Institute of Australia"],"a":2,"e":"FOKE, a resident group in the then Premier's electorate, used this phrase; it shows the second dominant representation of planning, alongside planning as a constraint on supply (pp. 14–15)."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"recall","q":"How did local governments and residents frame the problem with the planning system?","o":["As red tape that blocked housing supply","As too little growth in greenfield areas","As too few rights for developers to appeal","As distrust and undue developer influence"],"a":3,"e":"They spoke of 'politicised decision-making' and the 'undue influence of developers', fed by corruption investigations (p. 10). Red tape is the industry's framing."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"recall","q":"Which UK instrument do the authors cite as a planning tool for securing social housing in market developments?","o":["Single Regeneration Budget","Section 106 obligations","Urban growth boundaries","Business improvement zones"],"a":1,"e":"Under Thatcherism, councils could still use section 106 of the Town and Country Planning Act to secure land and contributions for social housing; the US equivalent discussed is inclusionary zoning (p. 4). SRB is from Raco and Imrie."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"recall","q":"Which materials did Gurran and Ruming compare in their discourse analysis?","o":["Selected submissions vs the White Paper and draft Bill","Interviews with planners vs official housing statistics","Newspaper articles vs parliamentary voting records","Developer annual accounts vs municipal planning permits"],"a":0,"e":"They selected 13 submissions representing the main interests and compared them, through close reading, coding and NVivo keyword searches, with the April 2013 White Paper and draft Planning Bill (pp. 8–9)."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"example","q":"What does the National Housing Supply Council's finding about social housing suggest, according to the authors?","o":["Planning delays were the main cause of the 2008 shortfall","Social housing crowded out private housing investment","Developers built more homes than the market could absorb","The gap reflected a public retreat, not planning failure"],"a":3,"e":"The estimated shortfall of about 85,000 dwellings would have been a modest surplus had social housing output stayed at its 1996 level (about 90,000 more homes) (p. 6), undercutting the claim that planning red tape caused the shortage."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"recall","q":"How did neoliberal reform change housing assistance in Australia, according to the article?","o":["Public housing stock was greatly expanded again","Rent control was introduced across the nation","Private rent subsidies replaced public housing","All land was nationalised for new housing"],"a":2,"e":"Public housing investment was replaced by subsidies for private renters; social housing approvals in NSW fell from about 15% of dwellings in 1983 to under 2% in 2014 (pp. 4–5)."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"not","q":"Which of the following was NOT one of the six reform areas the authors trace?","o":["Rent control in the private market","Spot rezoning and developer appeals","Community participation in planning","Infrastructure funding and charges"],"a":0,"e":"The six areas were spot rezoning, community consultation, decision speeds, red tape reduction, infrastructure funding and affordable housing (pp. 11–14); rent control is not among them."},{"t":"gurran","fmt":"contrast","q":"How did local governments and residents' view of planning objectives differ from the Property Council's?","o":["They wanted planning to deliver an economic dividend for NSW","They wanted social, environmental and heritage goals kept","They wanted all planning powers handed over to the Minister","They wanted infrastructure paid from general taxation"],"a":1,"e":"The PCA wanted planning 'geared towards delivering an economic dividend for NSW', whereas local government and residents wanted ecologically sustainable development, social and heritage objectives retained (pp. 10–11). General-tax infrastructure funding was an industry demand."}];
